[SPEAKER_02]: I think often people would understand that as like, precisely in this context, like food.

166
00:20:25,700 --> 00:20:32,162
[SPEAKER_02]: If I see a cake that has a nice shape or something, I shouldn't either...

167
00:20:32,942 --> 00:20:34,523
[SPEAKER_02]: Ideally, I shouldn't look at the cake at all.

168
00:20:35,623 --> 00:20:40,546
[SPEAKER_02]: If I look at it, I should somehow play with those perceptions, so that they don't...

169
00:20:40,566 --> 00:20:42,367
[SPEAKER_02]: I don't recognize, like, it's just a shape.

170
00:20:42,667 --> 00:20:45,148
[SPEAKER_02]: It's not really... really a delicious cake.

171
00:20:45,188 --> 00:20:46,108
[SPEAKER_02]: It's just a brown shape.

172
00:20:46,989 --> 00:20:56,274
[SPEAKER_02]: But, actually, the sign and feature is that theme, that you would... the theme might not be there at that moment.

173
00:20:57,514 --> 00:20:58,675
[SPEAKER_02]: You're suppressing it, let's say.

174
00:21:00,089 --> 00:21:03,553
[SPEAKER_02]: The theme of this food is agreeable, is nice.

175
00:21:04,193 --> 00:21:04,994
[SPEAKER_02]: I want more of it.

176
00:21:05,855 --> 00:21:07,296
[SPEAKER_02]: It's delightful.

177
00:21:07,336 --> 00:21:12,742
[SPEAKER_02]: That's not a kind of thing that you're telling yourself.

178
00:21:13,819 --> 00:21:22,485
[SPEAKER_02]: It's the implication of the thoughts that you will be having when you're actually acting out of craving for the cake.

179
00:21:23,465 --> 00:21:32,371
[SPEAKER_02]: Those would be the kind of themes that are underlying your whole mental activity around this thing.

180
00:21:32,732 --> 00:21:41,757
[SPEAKER_02]: But now, in this context, let's say, if somebody who just developed that blanket view of rejecting food, because food is rooted in craving,

181
00:21:42,678 --> 00:21:55,687
[SPEAKER_02]: The sign and feature there of the theme that they're developing, basically, or establishing themselves in, is a theme of aversion, theme of restlessness, theme of denial.

182
00:21:56,388 --> 00:22:05,975
[SPEAKER_02]: And they don't see that as a sign or feature that leads to the arising of states of distress, of agitation.

183
00:22:07,856 --> 00:22:23,047
[SPEAKER_02]: And so, that's what it means to... because, see, the sutta doesn't say... it doesn't look at sights on account of which lust and aversion would arise.

184
00:22:24,548 --> 00:22:28,851
[SPEAKER_02]: That's almost presupposed, if you're keeping virtue and have some common sense.

185
00:22:30,753 --> 00:22:32,594
[SPEAKER_02]: It's even much more subtle than that.

186
00:22:32,634 --> 00:22:34,575
[SPEAKER_02]: It's having seen a sight

187
00:22:35,436 --> 00:22:43,858
[SPEAKER_02]: Having heard a sound, having smelled a smell, he doesn't grasp at signs and features on account of which unwholesome states will arise.

188
00:22:44,418 --> 00:22:57,620
[SPEAKER_02]: So, it's... that's already at a level where you wouldn't be attending to those things, like, full-on, carelessly, because that already comes within the virtue, basically.

189
00:22:57,760 --> 00:22:58,960
[SPEAKER_02]: Like, it's just obvious.

190
00:22:59,021 --> 00:23:00,781
[SPEAKER_02]: This is taking you towards breaking the precept.

191
00:23:00,801 --> 00:23:01,281
[SPEAKER_02]: You wouldn't be

192
00:23:05,703 --> 00:23:30,376
[SPEAKER_02]: So, now, within that, when there are things that are, let's say, not unwholesome to look at, not unwholesome to smell, not unwholesome to taste, now you have to look at what are the themes, the background themes that I am maintaining in regard to this whatever object I'm engaging with.

193
00:23:33,026 --> 00:23:43,593
[SPEAKER_02]: Again, it's important to emphasize that it's something that becomes apparent.

194
00:23:43,793 --> 00:23:45,975
[SPEAKER_02]: It's pre-verbal, let's say.

195
00:23:45,995 --> 00:23:51,138
[SPEAKER_02]: It's not like a thing that you are saying to yourself.

196
00:23:51,418 --> 00:23:58,123
[SPEAKER_02]: It's the most kind of acute, subtle context behind this whole situation.

197
00:24:01,112 --> 00:24:08,984
[SPEAKER_02]: You could almost look at it as like... a very coarse example of it would be like the motivation when you're doing something.

198
00:24:09,565 --> 00:24:16,375
[SPEAKER_02]: Like if you're talking to someone and you're, let's say, using very polite language on the outside.

199
00:24:17,801 --> 00:24:34,712
[SPEAKER_02]: But actually, your ulterior motive there, you're not necessarily thinking that in your mind, like, that, let's say, I'm angry towards this person, and I really despise what they did, but I'm trying to...

200
00:24:34,932 --> 00:24:45,598
[SPEAKER_02]: I'm acting out of that anger, but I'm talking to them in a very polite way, because I hope that through that, they will not do that thing that I don't want them to do again.

201
00:24:46,679 --> 00:24:53,985
[SPEAKER_02]: So, that whole sign and feature, that whole theme, that you're not necessarily aware of, is a theme of aversion.

202
00:24:54,845 --> 00:25:01,510
[SPEAKER_02]: Even though, on that external level, you might not even be clear to yourself about the fact that that's the theme.

203
00:25:03,352 --> 00:25:14,320
[SPEAKER_02]: So, those are... that's what the Buddha meant with grasping signs and features, and in this context, going back to what we were describing, you would have to extend this principle to anything.

204
00:25:15,745 --> 00:25:21,926
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's how you would start to abandon these views, because you would realize, like, well, there's no value in this.

205
00:25:23,586 --> 00:25:34,688
[SPEAKER_02]: If, even if it's the right thing that I thought I got from the Buddha, there's no value in cultivating something that leads to the increase of lust or leads to the increase of aversion.

206
00:25:35,448 --> 00:25:40,029
[SPEAKER_02]: If it does, that means that's not what the Buddha was actually meant.

207
00:25:41,409 --> 00:25:44,010
[SPEAKER_02]: So, then you would start to see that

208
00:25:46,651 --> 00:25:55,833
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, the actual dispassion is a theme that's at that background level, on the level of mind, of the level of citta.

209
00:25:56,854 --> 00:26:13,778
[SPEAKER_02]: You start to realize, like, okay, there's absolutely no benefit if I am thinking thoughts about death, and thoughts about sickness, and thoughts about losing everything, if the mind, if that animal that's kind of indirectly

210
00:26:15,156 --> 00:26:18,177
[SPEAKER_02]: There at the same time, but but it's not the thoughts.

211
00:26:19,098 --> 00:26:28,962
[SPEAKER_02]: If that animal is getting actually agitated and less pliable on account of you thinking those thoughts.

212
00:26:33,364 --> 00:26:36,605
[SPEAKER_02]: Of course, it might be steadily kind of, um...

213
00:26:38,990 --> 00:26:47,912
[SPEAKER_02]: Off-putting to reflect on those things, but then that's something that you would begin to be able to distinguish for yourself.

214
00:26:47,952 --> 00:26:51,313
[SPEAKER_02]: Like, what's the right type of saṃvegā?

215
00:26:52,133 --> 00:26:56,214
[SPEAKER_02]: Of being moved by something, as the Buddha said, things that are worth being moved by.

216
00:26:57,314 --> 00:27:01,955
[SPEAKER_02]: What's the difference between that and just winding myself up for no for no purpose?

217
00:27:03,068 --> 00:27:08,513
[SPEAKER_02]: Because, you see, the wrong type of winding yourself up, let's say, would be more restlessness.

218
00:27:08,713 --> 00:27:17,200
[SPEAKER_02]: No, but actually, you would realize, like, if I stop doing this, I'm actually more inclined towards an wholesome stance than the opposite.

219
00:27:21,887 --> 00:27:22,668
[SPEAKER_03]: Can you say that again?

220
00:27:23,688 --> 00:27:36,917
[SPEAKER_02]: The wrong kind of working yourself... Like, if you're just thinking about death, thinking about sickness, thinking about aging... Like, the whole time, kind of... Yeah, but not paying any attention at all to, like, what is the attitude behind this.

221
00:27:37,698 --> 00:27:40,600
[SPEAKER_02]: You kind of just assume that that contemplation in itself is right.

222
00:27:41,160 --> 00:27:41,741
[SPEAKER_01]: Mechanically.

223
00:27:42,121 --> 00:27:42,961
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, exactly.

224
00:27:43,042 --> 00:27:45,183
[SPEAKER_02]: Like a mantra, almost.

225
00:27:46,704 --> 00:27:50,647
[SPEAKER_02]: You just... By doing that, you would be...

226
00:27:52,575 --> 00:27:55,637
[SPEAKER_02]: You would be putting yourself in a position... you're acting out of hindrances.

227
00:27:57,158 --> 00:28:04,483
[SPEAKER_02]: So, actually, your mind would be... like, right now, it's focused on this, because you're forcefully keeping it there.

228
00:28:05,403 --> 00:28:18,472
[SPEAKER_02]: But if you were to bring, let's say, let it loose and go wherever it wants, you would find that the unwholesome directions have been either maintained or maybe even increased, because you were acting out of the hindrances.

229
00:28:19,372 --> 00:28:28,675
[SPEAKER_02]: Whereas, if you were doing it with some recognition of like, okay, no, my mind was actually obsessed with life and intoxicated.

230
00:28:28,775 --> 00:28:33,776
[SPEAKER_02]: Intoxicated with life, intoxicated with youth, intoxicated with health, as a citizen would say.

231
00:28:34,317 --> 00:28:38,918
[SPEAKER_02]: And based on having recognized that theme, you cultivate this opposite theme.

232
00:28:39,799 --> 00:28:51,447
[SPEAKER_02]: Then, it's like, yeah, you might factually feel a sense of, like, urgency, a sense of disenchantment, even, like, almost, like, to the point of...

233
00:28:52,305 --> 00:28:57,530
[SPEAKER_02]: Of being moved by it, like, oh, I should be more heedful, I should be more careful with my actions.

234
00:28:57,991 --> 00:29:02,915
[SPEAKER_02]: But see, that is now actually taking you away from the hindrances.

235
00:29:03,116 --> 00:29:15,848
[SPEAKER_02]: It's leading you in the direction of... in the right direction, and it's... even if it's not, let's say, perfectly peaceful, as it would be to just ignore that you would... that you are subject to these things,

236
00:29:17,038 --> 00:29:22,700
[SPEAKER_02]: It is leading to a decrease of unwholesome states overall.

237
00:29:23,581 --> 00:29:35,045
[SPEAKER_02]: So, that's why... it's like... that simile of the cook, actually, the Chinese version, has a very nice passage that doesn't occur in the Pali one.

238
00:29:35,745 --> 00:29:40,527
[SPEAKER_02]: And actually, it was the very thing that we were discussing in that sign of the mind talk.

239
00:29:41,287 --> 00:29:42,608
[SPEAKER_02]: It's like, if the cook

240
00:29:43,947 --> 00:29:55,250
[SPEAKER_02]: presents a dish to the king based on his own ideas, his preconceived notions of what the king should be eating.

241
00:29:56,320 --> 00:30:11,025
[SPEAKER_02]: Rather than actually paying attention to the preferences of the king and where he inclines, then that's why he will never be able to basically be a good cook and get promoted and rewarded by the king.

242
00:30:11,825 --> 00:30:25,270
[SPEAKER_02]: So, it's that same thing like you have, on account of reading, on account of contemplating, you have developed a blanket view that this food, that this type of contemplation is good because, you know, it's about death, it's about

243
00:30:25,925 --> 00:30:26,326
[SPEAKER_02]: Sickness.

244
00:30:26,446 --> 00:30:38,364
[SPEAKER_02]: Okay, sure, there are probably worse things to think about, but if you do it without paying any attention to where you're at, your whole attitude behind it, and you're not even aware, like I said,

245
00:30:39,432 --> 00:30:47,774
[SPEAKER_02]: That there was some intoxication, that there was some wrong attitude there, and you're just doing it because the Buddha said I should do this.

246
00:30:48,355 --> 00:30:49,595
[SPEAKER_04]: You are cooking for yourself.

247
00:30:49,675 --> 00:30:52,156
[SPEAKER_04]: Exactly.

248
00:30:52,776 --> 00:30:53,216
[SPEAKER_02]: Exactly.

249
00:30:54,436 --> 00:30:56,377
[SPEAKER_02]: So, that's why you want to...

250
00:30:56,797 --> 00:31:06,660
[SPEAKER_02]: It doesn't matter what the contemplation is, especially when you're not sufficiently familiar with the science of your mind and you haven't got the right view yet.

251
00:31:06,700 --> 00:31:07,000
[SPEAKER_02]: You should

252
00:31:08,122 --> 00:31:11,692
[SPEAKER_02]: Your main contemplation should be like, what's my attitude behind this?

253
00:31:13,182 --> 00:31:20,545
[SPEAKER_02]: Because that's what will keep you in check and see if you are doubtful, like, well, I'm not sure what my attitude behind this is.

254
00:31:21,145 --> 00:31:35,211
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, you should actually redirect your effort to clarifying that most of the time, rather than just continuing to... because what are you going to be making foods, like, cooking dishes with... based on probability?

255
00:31:35,431 --> 00:31:38,653
[SPEAKER_02]: Like, oh, I think this one is more likely to

256
00:31:39,433 --> 00:31:46,176
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, no, it's better to actually... okay, well, instead of doing this, let me look at the king and see what's actually going on.

257
00:31:47,477 --> 00:31:48,877
[SPEAKER_01]: What the mind really needs?

258
00:31:49,918 --> 00:31:58,962
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, like, what the mind needs in terms of... if it has been inclining in... you actually see it inclining in the wrong direction.

259
00:31:58,982 --> 00:32:03,344
[SPEAKER_02]: And you realize, okay, now contemplating death will take it away from that wrong direction.

260
00:32:04,479 --> 00:32:12,387
[SPEAKER_02]: But if you just don't know, and you just keep doing it, you will be... inevitably, you will be winding yourself up unnecessarily.

261
00:32:13,048 --> 00:32:19,274
[SPEAKER_02]: Because there is no need to make effort, unless there are unwholesome states present.

262
00:32:19,314 --> 00:32:21,897
[SPEAKER_02]: That becomes your unwholesome state, your restlessness.

263
00:32:26,537 --> 00:32:28,798
[SPEAKER_02]: So, that's exactly what the Buddha said.

264
00:32:29,278 --> 00:32:30,799
[SPEAKER_02]: Like, you have four right strivings.

265
00:32:31,339 --> 00:32:34,661
[SPEAKER_02]: You have abandoning arisen unwholesome states.

266
00:32:35,121 --> 00:32:39,223
[SPEAKER_02]: You have making sure that unwholesome states don't arise.

267
00:32:40,104 --> 00:32:46,026
[SPEAKER_02]: You have making sure that wholesome states arise and making sure that wholesome states are maintained.

268
00:32:46,707 --> 00:32:48,407
[SPEAKER_02]: So, that's an effort in and of itself.

269
00:32:49,328 --> 00:32:50,949
[SPEAKER_02]: And if you only do the other three,

270
00:32:52,854 --> 00:33:09,808
[SPEAKER_02]: Something's going to be wrong, because sometimes you have a, at least, more wholesome state of mind, where there isn't anything harmful at the time, and you need to be able to recognize that, and learn how to protect it, rather than constantly assuming, like, no, no, no, there must be something unwholesome.

271
00:33:15,393 --> 00:33:16,854
[SPEAKER_02]: It's like, no, the king needs more food.

272
00:33:18,867 --> 00:33:20,108
[SPEAKER_02]: Just by default, he needs.

273
00:33:20,468 --> 00:33:22,489
[SPEAKER_02]: He needs more.

274
00:33:22,650 --> 00:33:23,110
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, no.

275
00:33:23,830 --> 00:33:26,372
[SPEAKER_02]: Sometimes, he just had enough and that's good for now.

